  <section class="page">
    <div class="prose-block col-full">

      <h2 class="col-head" id="task">The task</h2>
      <p class="col-prose">For the 5EID0 “Venus Project” (Group 10, 2026) a PYNQ-Z2 rover has to hunt colored cubes, map terrain, and coordinate with a partner robot over one MQTT link — UART0 to an ESP32 to the broker. The firmware is C, runs from boot, and I also wrote the Python mission-control tool.</p>
      <p class="col-prose">Two rovers start back to back at the origin of an arena bounded by black tape, with cubes of two sizes, <span class="nb">3 cm</span> and <span class="nb">6 cm</span>, and obstacles we called mountains. The job is to find all six cubes, report each with its position, size, color and the local temperature, and stop. No operator and no keyboard: the firmware starts with no arguments and talks only over MQTT, so the robot has to be sure of what it has seen before it acts on it.</p>
      <p class="col-prose">The report’s non-functional requirements set the tempo: the distance array had to refresh the obstacle logic at ≥ <span class="nb">10 Hz</span>, a cliff or boundary had to stop forward motion within <span class="nb">200 ms</span>, and the map had to update within <span class="nb">1 s</span> of a packet. The wireless link is an ESP32-C3-WROOM-02 connectivity board on UART, speaking MQTT to the broker; only wireless communication was allowed during the mission (Fig.&nbsp;2).</p>

