      <h2 class="col-head" id="sensors">Sensors and odometry</h2>
      <aside class="note col-margin">1600 steps/rev · Ø75 mm · 120 mm track</aside>
      <p class="col-prose">A vertical stack of three VL53L0X time-of-flight sensors sits on a shared I²C bus: the bottom beam for <span class="nb">3 cm</span> cubes, the middle for <span class="nb">6 cm</span> cubes, the top for obstacles — “mountains” (Fig.&nbsp;3). Two TCRT5000 IR sensors watch for the black-tape boundary, a TCS3200 reads color, and an NTC thermistor reads temperature. Two stepper-driven wheels give differential drive, with dead-reckoned odometry from the commanded steps — no encoders: <span class="nb">1600 steps/rev</span>, <span class="nb">Ø75 mm</span> wheels, <span class="nb">120 mm</span> track.</p>
      <p class="col-prose">Every VL53L0X ships at the same I²C address, <span class="mono">0x29</span>, so the bus is sorted out at boot: the firmware holds the middle and bottom sensors in shutdown through their XSHUT pins, finds the always-on top sensor and moves it to <span class="mono">0x68</span>, then wakes the other two one at a time and re-addresses them to <span class="mono">0x69</span> and <span class="mono">0x6A</span>.</p>

      <figure class="fig fig--right" id="fig-3">
