        <figcaption id="fig-3-cap"><span class="fig__no">Fig.&nbsp;3</span> — Three time-of-flight sensors on one shared I²C bus, one beam per height: bottom for 3 cm cubes, middle for 6 cm cubes, top for obstacles. Cubes drawn to scale; sensor heights schematic.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="col-prose">The XSHUT bring-up was verified with an I²C bus scanner after each boot phase: one device at <span class="mono">0x29</span>, then <span class="mono">0x68</span> and <span class="mono">0x29</span>, then <span class="mono">0x69</span> joins, ending at a stable <span class="mono">0x68 / 0x69 / 0x6A</span>. Settling delays below <span class="nb">50 ms</span> caused boot-timing collisions, which is why the firmware waits <span class="nb">100 ms</span>. Disconnecting AR4 or AR5 to simulate a dead sensor made the driver log a non-fatal timeout and carry on without locking the bus.</p>
      <p class="col-prose">The NTC divider — <span class="nb">10 kΩ</span>, <span class="nb">3.3 V</span>, <span class="nb">β = 4050 K</span> — first read wrong because the ADC’s input impedance loaded the divider; a unity-gain op-amp buffer between divider and ADC fixed it. The TCS3200 is read as a frequency: S0/S1 at <span class="nb">20 %</span> scaling, calibrated against white and black, <span class="nb">20</span> static readings per cube, classified by Euclidean distance to five color profiles. It is accurate at the <span class="nb">15–20 mm</span> working distance and degrades with range. Fig.&nbsp;4 shows how the sensors, the control loop and the link fit together, with the interfaces as they are actually wired.</p>

