      <aside class="pull">
        <p class="pull__kicker">verification</p>
        <p>A cube is a cube when at least two of six reads agree within <span class="nb">5 cm</span>.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="col-head" id="autopilot">The autopilot</h2>
      <p class="col-prose">Four reactive states: SCAN, turning in <span class="nb">5°</span> steps, <span class="nb">72</span> per revolution, looking for the nearest unknown cube on the beams; APPROACH, driving toward a remembered cube and steering around a mountain if one is closer; EVALUATE, reading the color, registering and reporting the cube, then backing off <span class="nb">5 cm</span>; and ROAM, turning a random direction and driving <span class="nb">40 cm</span> when a full sweep finds nothing. A boundary-evade interrupt is checked at the top of every tick and pre-empts all four (Fig.&nbsp;1).</p>
      <p class="col-prose">The approach switches to an open-loop final drive inside <span class="nb">10 cm</span>, because the time-of-flight sensors cannot read below about <span class="nb">6 cm</span>, and gives up after <span class="nb">30</span> ticks rather than pushing forever. When the IR sensors see tape, the evade reverses, turns about <span class="nb">180°</span> with <span class="nb">±30°</span> of jitter, drops any approach in progress, and re-scans.</p>

      {photos && (
      <figure class="fig fig--right" id="fig-5">
        <img class="fig__img fig__img--portrait" src="../assets/work/venus-bench-front.jpg" width="1125" height="2000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The rover from the front on a lab bench: two wheels, the PYNQ-Z2 in its case with a breadboard shield, the 3D-printed sensor mount with two IR sensor boards angled outward, the color sensor board with four white LEDs and the stacked time-of-flight boards">
        <figcaption><span class="fig__no">Fig.&nbsp;5</span> — The rover from the front on a lab bench: the PYNQ-Z2 in its case with a breadboard shield, the 3D-printed sensor mount with the two IR boards, the color sensor board and the stacked time-of-flight boards. June 2026.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      )}

      <h2 class="col-head" id="dropout">What survives a dropout</h2>
      <p class="col-prose">Time-of-flight reads drop out, so a cube is confirmed by a 6-read burst vote: at least two reads within <span class="nb">5 cm</span>. Color is averaged over <span class="nb">20</span> reads, and a cube is registered on detection strength, so a weak color read never discards a real find. Finds are deduplicated within <span class="nb">12 cm</span>. The thresholds were tuned against on-bench ToF readings, and the README says so, so the next person can re-measure rather than guess.</p>
      <p class="col-prose">Both robots share one world frame — same origin, back-to-back headings — so a cube position from either robot is comparable with no transform at run time. Every new find goes out as a JSON frame over UART0 to the ESP32, which relays it to the broker; the partner drains its UART each tick and folds any find with a known color into its own registry. When the sixth cube is known, by either robot, both stop.</p>

      <h2 class="col-head" id="mission-control">Mission control</h2>
      <p class="col-prose">The Python mission-control tool is an MQTT relay between the two robots, a JSON message logger, and a live matplotlib map of both robots’ paths, headings and every cube, drawn to scale — <span class="nb">3 cm</span> against <span class="nb">6 cm</span>.</p>
      <h2 class="col-head" id="revisions">Six revisions</h2>
      <p class="col-prose">The firmware went through six revisions. The third is the structural one: the sensor code, the ToF driver and the MQTT framing that had lived in separate files were consolidated into a single <span class="mono">main.c</span> of about <span class="nb">2,600</span> lines that links against only libpynq and libm, so deployment is one <span class="mono">gcc</span> command and a copy.</p>

